 "blocks": [
  {
   "type": "text",
   "kind": "credits",
   "html": "<p>Tools: Rhino, Grasshopper (C#), TetGen</p><p>Reference: Carbon Design Engine</p><p>Status: Design tool, 2025 – ongoing</p><p>Role: Built the whole pipeline alone: point seeding, tetrahedralization, surface extraction, cell typing, and strut generation.<br>Designed the cell-transition scheme that lets one part carry several lattice types without seams.<br>Modeled the application studies: bicycle saddle, VR headset gasket, headphone earpads.</p>",
   "layout": "credits"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "kind": "quote",
   "layout": "quote",
   "html": "<h2>What if a cushion could be grown to fit the body that sits on it?</h2>"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "html": "<p>Lattice++ is a lattice generation system I built inside Grasshopper, taking Carbon's Design Engine as the reference. Give it any closed geometry and it fills the volume with a conformal lattice: tetrahedral, hexahedral, or Voronoi cells, at a chosen density and strut thickness. Density, thickness, and even the cell type can change gradually across one part. It is a design tool, not yet a simulation tool. But the shapes it makes are the shapes that could one day be tuned to a person's own pressure map.</p>",
   "kind": "text"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "kind": "section",
   "layout": "section",
   "html": "<h2>The tool</h2>"
  },
  {
   "type": "row",
   "columns": [
    {
     "flex": 0.5,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "type": "image",
       "src": "lattice/lattice_19_slide-tool-goal.jpg",
       "width": 1920,
       "height": 1080,
       "lqip": "data:image/jpeg;base64,",
       "tone": "#fefefe",
       "caption": "Any closed geometry in; struts, nodes, and a printable mesh out.",
       "kind": "white"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "flex": 0.5,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "type": "image",
       "src": "lattice/lattice_18_slide-seven-types.jpg",
       "width": 1920,
       "height": 1080,
       "lqip": "data:image/jpeg;base64,",
       "tone": "#fcfcfc",
       "caption": "Seven cell families on the same torus: tetrahedral, rhombic, icosahedral, Voronoi, kagome, star, Kelvin.",
       "kind": "white"
      }
     ]
    }
   ],
   "kind": "row"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "html": "<h3>How it works</h3><p>The tool builds on two kinds of grid. <strong>The hexahedral grid</strong> is the simple one: a cube, extruded, gridded, or lofted through the volume, with the same unit cell repeated inside and the cells at the boundary cut to the shape. It is regular and predictable, but it only approximates the surface.</p>",
   "kind": "text"
  },
  {
   "type": "row",
   "columns": [
    {
     "flex": 0.5,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "type": "image",
       "src": "lattice/lattice_22_slide-hex-torus.jpg",
       "width": 1920,
       "height": 1080,
       "lqip": "data:image/jpeg;base64,",
       "tone": "#dbebed",
       "caption": "Hexahedral grid on a torus.",
       "kind": "white"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "flex": 0.5,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "type": "image",
       "src": "lattice/lattice_21_slide-hex-cells.jpg",
       "width": 1920,
       "height": 1080,
       "lqip": "data:image/jpeg;base64,",
       "tone": "#fdfdfd",
       "caption": "Hex cells built by extrusion, grid, or loft, with the same star cell inside.",
       "kind": "white"
      }
     ]
    }
   ],
   "kind": "row"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "html": "<p><strong>The tetrahedral grid</strong> is what makes the lattice conformal. Points are seeded at a controllable density, and TetGen, called from a C# component, fills the volume with well-shaped tetrahedra. The outer faces of that mesh are extracted, so the lattice follows the input surface exactly.</p>",
   "layout": "text-l",
   "kind": "text"
  },
  {
   "type": "row",
   "columns": [
    {
     "flex": 0.5,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "type": "image",
       "src": "lattice/lattice_20_slide-tetgen-wip.jpg",
       "width": 1920,
       "height": 1080,
       "lqip": "data:image/jpeg;base64,",
       "tone": "#fefefe",
       "caption": "An early tetrahedralization: usable, but with a few small and distorted cells near the boundary.",
       "kind": "white"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "flex": 0.5,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "type": "image",
       "src": "lattice/lattice_15_tetgen-workflow.jpg",
       "width": 2400,
       "height": 1344,
       "lqip": "data:image/jpeg;base64,",
       "tone": "#f5f5f5",
       "caption": "TetGen inside Grasshopper: the tetrahedralization step, before and after refinement.",
       "kind": "white"
      }
     ]
    }
   ],
   "kind": "row"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "html": "<p>Cell types are assigned per vertex rather than per cell, so a transition happens inside a cell, not between cells. That is what lets one part carry several lattice types without a seam.</p>",
   "layout": "text-l",
   "kind": "text"
  },
  {
   "type": "row",
   "columns": [
    {
     "flex": 0.5,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "type": "image",
       "src": "lattice/lattice_23_slide-transition-cell.jpg",
       "width": 1920,
       "height": 1080,
       "lqip": "data:image/jpeg;base64,",
       "tone": "#fefefe",
       "caption": "Each vertex of a tetrahedron carries a cell type, or a mix of types. The cell between them blends.",
       "kind": "white"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "flex": 0.5,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "type": "image",
       "src": "lattice/lattice_24_slide-zones-wip.jpg",
       "width": 1920,
       "height": 1080,
       "lqip": "data:image/jpeg;base64,",
       "tone": "#fbfbfb",
       "caption": "Assigning cell types per node in a custom script, next to the brute-force zoning a native component gives.",
       "kind": "white"
      }
     ]
    }
   ],
   "kind": "row"
  },
  {
   "type": "row",
   "columns": [
    {
     "flex": 0.5,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "type": "image",
       "src": "lattice/lattice_25_slide-zone-icosa-tetra.jpg",
       "width": 1920,
       "height": 1080,
       "lqip": "data:image/jpeg;base64,",
       "tone": "#fbfbfb",
       "caption": "Zone transition: icosahedral to tetrahedral.",
       "kind": "white"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "flex": 0.5,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "type": "image",
       "src": "lattice/lattice_26_slide-zone-voronoi.jpg",
       "width": 1920,
       "height": 1080,
       "lqip": "data:image/jpeg;base64,",
       "tone": "#fcfcfc",
       "caption": "Zone transition: Voronoi.",
       "kind": "white"
      }
     ]
    }
   ],
   "kind": "row"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "html": "<h3>Three gradients</h3><p>Density follows the point seeding, so it can thin out where a part needs to be light and tighten where it needs support. Strut thickness is a per-edge value and can follow any field. Cell type is the unusual one: because types live on vertices, a part can go from a stiff Kelvin cell to a soft Voronoi cell continuously.</p>",
   "kind": "text"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "kind": "section",
   "layout": "section",
   "html": "<h2>Studies</h2>"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "html": "<p>A saddle and a pair of earpads: two places where a body meets a product and a single foam density is always a compromise.</p>",
   "layout": "text-l",
   "kind": "text"
  },
  {
   "type": "image",
   "src": "lattice/lattice_04_saddle-front-frame.jpg",
   "width": 1600,
   "height": 1333,
   "lqip": "data:image/jpeg;base64,",
   "tone": "#ffffff",
   "caption": "Saddle on its rails. Hover to see the lattice alone.",
   "hover": {
